// Reading an existing value at a **union** type argument — a `C<Dog>` as a `C<Dog | Cat>` — where
// the declaration makes it sound. It is the same widening as reading it at a `dyn Trait` or at the
// open `dyn`, and the declaration decides all of them the same way: a widened view is unsafe only
// where a value of the wider type can be written back through it into something whose own type says
// `Dog`.
//
// The two other shapes the union argument has to keep working are here too, because both are
// decided by the same arm and neither is a widening:
//
//   * **Reflexivity**, `Box<Dog | Cat>` read as a `Box<Dog | Cat>`. `Box` is invariant, so this pair
//     is decided without forming a widening anywhere: the two unions match by their member sets. A
//     rule that decomposed it into `Dog <: Dog | Cat` would refuse a value being read at its own
//     type.
//   * **Member order**, `Box<Dog | Cat>` read as a `Box<Cat | Dog>`. A union is its set of members,
//     so the two spellings name one type and either reaches the other.
//
// Each widened view is used afterwards, because a widening that type-checks and yields the wrong
// value is the failure this file exists to exclude. The last three lines store through the original
// `Box` and read the two views back: they alias it, and the store is a `Cat` into a slot both views
// already call `Dog | Cat`, which is what makes the aliasing sound rather than merely permitted.
// expect: stdout "dog"
// expect: stdout "woof"
// expect: stdout "dog"
// expect: stdout "dog"
// expect: stdout "cat"
// expect: stdout "cat"
// expect: exit 0
trait Speak {
    fn speak(): string
}

struct Dog {
    impl Speak {
        pub fn speak(): string {
            return "woof"
        }
    }
}

struct Cat {
    impl Speak {
        pub fn speak(): string {
            return "meow"
        }
    }
}

// A value struct: even a `mut` field is a read position as far as the ORIGINAL is concerned, because
// a struct field-set rebinds the binding rather than writing through a shared object.
struct Cell<T> {
    mut v: T
}

// A reference class whose field is immutable and whose method only hands the parameter out.
class Reader<T> {
    v: T

    pub fn new(v: T): Reader<T> {
        return Reader { v: v }
    }

    pub fn get(): T {
        return self.v
    }
}

// A reference class with a `mut` field of the parameter's type: invariant, so every pair below that
// mentions it relates two `Box`es of the SAME argument.
class Box<T> {
    pub mut v: T

    pub fn new(v: T): Box<T> {
        return Box { v: v }
    }
}

// Takes the union, so calling it through a widened view is what proves the view carries the wider
// static type rather than merely being accepted.
fn kind_of(x: Dog | Cat): string {
    if x is Dog {
        return "dog"
    }
    return "cat"
}

c: Cell<Dog> = Cell { v: Dog {} }
widened_cell: Cell<Dog | Cat> = c
echo kind_of(widened_cell.v)
echo c.v.speak()

r: Reader<Dog> = Reader.new(Dog {})
widened_reader: Reader<Dog | Cat> = r
echo kind_of(widened_reader.get())

// The construction the refusal's help line points at: a `Box<Dog>` cannot be read as a
// `Box<Dog | Cat>`, and a `Box` built where the wider type is stated instantiates its argument at
// the union directly.
b: Box<Dog | Cat> = Box.new(Dog {})
echo kind_of(b.v)

same: Box<Dog | Cat> = b
reordered: Box<Cat | Dog> = b
b.v = Cat {}
echo kind_of(same.v)
echo kind_of(reordered.v)
